Reviewer: 2

Recommendation: It appears that publication in any form would be premature at this time.

Comments:
Dear Editor,

In the following, you can find my review of the manuscript "When is committee force uncertainty worth computing in MD? Decision quality, cheap gates, and the baselines that must be beaten".
My recommendation is to reject the manuscript. A short summary of the manuscript as well as the reasons for my recommendation can be found below.

The paper discusses the evaluation of committee uncertainties and different strategies to further process them in workflows such as query by committee.
The key results are:
- A comparison against the actual error is important
- The selection rule for structures in a query by committee workflow should be size independent
- A structure-wide average of force uncertainty performs better than choosing the structure with the largest disagreement of a single atom or a single force component.
- Given the additional cost associated with committee force disagreements, the relevant benchmark to compare these strategies against should not be limited to the random selection of structures, but also include essentially-free metrics.

The manuscript suffers from many problems:
- Some of the basic claims are - to my knowledge - not true, or at least not sufficiently referenced. The author claims that a comparison between the uncertainty metric and true error is uncommon in the field of uncertainty quantification and instead cheap uncertainty metrics are measured against more expensive ones. The author further claims that these uncertainty metrics are strongly size dependent. Neither of these claims are supported by the given references. In fact, there is only a single reference to another uncertainty-focused work and neither of the two claims are supported by it.
- Across most sections, the manuscript is incomprehensible. It uses terminology that is completely uncommon in the field. In the methods section it introduces methodology and concepts in very broad strokes and without any references to more detailed work. It excessively uses pronouns and "the former", "others" and similar constructions in a way that makes it impossible to identify what is being talked about in each sentence.
- The manuscript lacks a proper introduction to the field and references to highly relevant work. A manuscript working on the topic of shallow ensembles/multi-head committees should at the very least cite papers such as doi.org/10.1088/2632-2153/ad594a or doi.org/10.1088/2632-2153/ae0bf0, as well as the review and papers contained in the review doi.org/10.1515/revce-2024-0028 to evaluate the performance of uncertainty estimates. The full list of relevant omitted publications is much longer and up to the author to collect.

On a more detailed level, I also found these shortcomings:
- Figures 1 and 2 compare 3BPA disjoint, 3BPA overlapping, 3BPA same, ethanol, aspirin and azobenzene. Why are the first three system + committee type and the last three just a system? What committee type was used? Why change what is being compared mid-figure?
- Table 1 shows that the force magnitude is the best "uncertainty estimate", beating every pooling of committee disagreement as well as the energy disagreement. Given the experimental setup, this revolutionary result does not hold up to scrutiny. It is stated that the model used was the MACE-MP foundation model with disjoint committee heads. This model was trained on the same MPtraj dataset used for testing. Furthermore, the dataset contains many crystal structures at equilibrium, where forces are exactly 0. This has massive implications on a test that uses force magnitude as an "uncertainty estimate". Neither are these aspects discussed, nor are the results tested on an out-of-domain test set.

In conclusion, the manuscript shows large problems in many core areas, such as knowledge of the state of the art of the field, the execution of the scientific experiments, the presentation of the data and the writing in general. The key results of the manuscript beyond these issues are the comparison of different pooling techniques for query by committee, the comparison against essentially-free metrics and an analysis of the effects of reducing the committee size of the uncertainty metric. These results of some interest to the field of uncertainty quantification in atomistic simulations.
However, even after a hypothetical major revision that rectifies all issues, the results would still not be of sufficient relevance to justify a publication in a journal such as JCTC.

